[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:Too High a Price?]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.24]Lesson 47 | 第47课
[00:02.45]Too high a price? | 代价太高？
[00:09.98]What does the writer describe as an 'amusing old-fashioned source of noise' ? | 作者把什么描述为“有趣的老式噪音来源”？
[00:18.65]Pollution is the price we pay for an overpopulated, overindustrialized planet. | 污染就是我们为这个人口过剩、过度工业化的星球所付出的代价。
[00:25.49]When you come to think about it, there are only four ways you can deal with rubbish: | 仔细想一想，处理垃圾只有四种方法：
[00:30.76]dump it, burn it, turn it into something you can use again, attempt to produce less of it. | 倾倒、焚烧、将其变成可再利用的东西、设法减少其产生量。
[00:37.73]We keep trying all four methods, but sheer volume of rubbish we produce worldwide threatens to overwhelm us. | 这四种方法我们都在不断尝试，但我们在全世界范围内产生的垃圾量之大，大有把我们淹没之势。
[00:48.10]Rubbish, however, is only part of the problem of polluting our planet. | 然而，垃圾只是污染我们这个星球的问题的一部分。
[00:53.50]The need to produce ever increasing quantities of cheap food leads to a different kind of pollution. | 对日益增多的廉价食品的需求导致了另一种类型的污染。
[01:00.99]Industriallized farming metheods produce cheap meat products: beef, pork and chicken. | 工业化的农业方式生产出廉价的肉制品：牛肉、猪肉和鸡肉。
[01:08.21]The use of pesticides and fertilizers produces cheap grain and vegetables. | 杀虫剂和化肥的使用生产出廉价的粮食和蔬菜。
[01:14.44]The price we pay for cheap food may be already too high: | 我们为廉价食品付出的代价可能已经太高：
[01:19.63]Mad Cow Disease (BSE) in cattle, salmonella in chicken and eggs, and listeria in dairy products. | 牛群中的疯牛病、鸡肉和鸡蛋中的沙门氏菌、以及乳制品中的李斯特杆菌。
[01:29.65]And if you think you'll abandon meat and become a vegetarian, | 而如果你打算放弃肉食成为素食者，
[01:33.86]you have the choice of very expensive organically-grown vegetables | 那么你可以选择非常昂贵的有机蔬菜，
[01:38.89]or a steady diet of pesticides every time you think you're eating fresh salads and vegetables, | 或者每当你自以为在吃新鲜沙拉和蔬菜时，实际吃下的是农药，
[01:45.59]or just having an innocent glass of water! | 或者仅仅喝一杯无害的水！
[01:49.60]However, there is an even more insidious kind of pollution that particularly affects urban area and invades our daily lives, and that is noise. | 然而，还有一种更加隐蔽的污染，它特别影响城市地区，并侵入我们的日常生活，那就是噪音。
[02:02.48]Burglar alarms going off at any time of the day or night serve only to annoy passers-by and actually assist burglars to burgle. | 防盗警报器在白天或夜晚的任何时候响起，只会惹恼过路人，实际上反倒帮助了窃贼行窃。
[02:13.10]Car alarms constantly scream at us in the street and are a source of profound irritation. | 汽车警报器在街上不停地朝我们尖啸，是令人深感恼火的根源。
[02:20.09]A recent survey of the effects of noise revealed (surprisingly?) | 最近一项关于噪音影响的调查揭示（令人惊讶吗？），
[02:25.37]that dogs barking incessantly in the night rated the highest form of noise pollution on a scale ranging from 1 to 7. | 在1到7级的量表上，夜里不停地吠叫的狗被评为最高级别的噪音污染。
[02:35.37]The survey revealed a large number of sources of noise that we really dislike. | 调查揭示了许许多多我们真正讨厌的噪音来源。
[02:41.79]Lawn mowers whining on a summer's day, late-night parties in apartment blocks, | 夏日里呜呜作响的割草机、公寓楼里深夜的聚会、
[02:47.97]noisy neighbours, vehicles of all kinds, | 吵闹的邻居、各种各样的车辆、
[02:51.51]especially large container trucks thundering through quiet villages, | 特别是轰鸣着穿过安静村庄的大型集装箱卡车、
[02:56.23]planes and helicopters flying overhead, large radios carried round in public places and played at maximum volume. | 在头顶上飞过的飞机和直升机、以及被人带到公共场所以最大音量播放的大型收音机。
[03:06.30]New technology has also made its own contribution to noise. | 新技术也为噪音作出了自己的贡献。
[03:11.44]A lot of people object to mobile phones, especially when they are used in public places like restaurant or on public transport. | 许多人反对使用移动电话，尤其是在餐馆或公共交通工具上等公共场所使用。
[03:21.69]Loud conversations on mobile phones invade our thoughts or interrupt the pleasure of meeting friends for a quiet chat. | 移动电话上的大声交谈侵入我们的思绪，或打断了与朋友安静聊天的乐趣。
[03:31.17]The noise pollution survey revealed a rather surprising and possibly amusing old-fashioned source of noise. | 噪音污染调查揭示了一个相当令人惊讶、可能还有趣的老式噪音来源。
[03:39.90]It turned out to be snoring! Men were found to be the worst offenders. | 它竟然是打呼噜！男人被发现是最严重的肇事者。
[03:46.22]It was revealed that 20% of men in their mid-thirties snore. | 据透露，三十五岁左右的男人中有20%打呼噜。
[03:52.06]This figure rises to a staggering 60% of men in their sixties. | 这个数字到六十多岁的男人中上升到惊人的60%。
[03:58.26]Against these figures, it was found that only 5% of women snore regularly, | 与这些数字相对照，只有5%的女性经常打呼噜，
[04:04.52]while the rest are constantly woken or kept awake by their trumpeting partners. | 其余的女性则不断被她们那 trumpeting 般打呼的伴侣吵醒或弄得无法入睡。
[04:11.34]Whatever the source of noise, one thing is certain: silence, it seems, has become a golden memory. | 无论噪音来自何处，有一点是肯定的：宁静似乎已成为金色的记忆。
